      <h1>Controversy</h1>

      <p>Most of what follows comes from a roughly hour-and-a-half documentary by the YouTuber Turkey Tom, which walks through Goatis' life and online career from his teenage years to the present day. It leans on old news articles, archived videos of Goatis himself, reviews from Airbnb hosts, screenshots, and a large number of testimonies from former partners, acquaintances and anonymous forum posters.</p>

      <p class="note">A word of caution: these are very different kinds of evidence. Some points below are Goatis' own words on camera. Others are claims by people with an axe to grind, or anonymous posts that nobody can verify. Where the video presents something as an allegation, so does this page. Goatis has been charged with none of the most serious accusations below, as far as the video reports.</p>

      <h2>The school incident (2006)</h2>

      <p>The story starts in Latvia. According to a 2006 Latvian news article cited in the video, a tenth-grade student named "Gatis L." lost control at his school and slashed at four schoolmates with a knife. The injuries were minor; all four were treated and sent home.</p>

      <p>Police quoted in the article put it down to past bullying and humiliation, and said the student had been fixated on a film about the Columbine shooting. Years later, British tabloids describing his stunts in the UK reported that he had a criminal record in Latvia over the incident.</p>

      <p>Around the same time, his own YouTube profile said he hated humans and enjoyed reading about mass murders and school shootings, which did not help matters.</p>

      <h2>Fasting, sungazing and the psychiatric ward (around 2014)</h2>

      <p>After moving to Berlin, Goatis went through a spiritual and conspiracy-theory phase. By his own account on video, he combined hours of sungazing with an extreme fast of a month or more, after which he could barely walk. Police took him to hospital at under 100 pounds. Because he kept pulling out his feeding tube, doctors assumed he was anorexic and moved him to a psychiatric ward, where he stayed for around two months.</p>

      <p>He has said he doesn't regret it and called it one of the clearest moments of his life, while advising viewers not to copy him.</p>

      <h2>From vegan to raw meat (2015 onward)</h2>

      <p>Goatis' diet moved from veganism to keto to raw meat and eggs. Within months he got food poisoning from raw bone marrow. He blamed the butcher, specifically "chemtrails". Undeterred, he moved on to raw poultry, raw fish and deliberately aged, rotting "high meat".</p>

      <p>This is also when the stunts that made him famous began. Inspired by another YouTuber who had eaten a raw cow heart at a vegan protest, he ate a raw goat's head at a vegan festival. Later performances included a pig's head and a squirrel in Australia and the UK, and a necklace made of a dead bird. The public, and vegans in particular, were not won over.</p>

      <MediaVideo
        src="/media/controversy-raw-poultry.mp4" :aspect="9 / 16"
        title="Raw poultry at a vegan event"
        caption="An edit of a shirtless Goatis, covered in fake blood eating raw poultry at a vegan event."
      />
      <p>The stunts made national news in the UK. He was fined £400 for disturbing the peace, said openly that he would neither pay nor show up to court, and left the country. As a result, he can't return without risking arrest.</p>

      <h2>In his own words</h2>

      <p>A large part of the documentary simply plays Goatis' older videos back. In them he argues that killing may be natural and therefore not wrong, says he wanted to bite and eat a doctor during his hospital stay, and calls abuse within relationships "superficial" if the love is real.</p>

      <p>Video titles from this era include claims that cooking food is satanic, that nobody is obese, and that school is a prison for deformed slaves. He also claimed he would beat the strongman Hafþór Björnsson in a fight "100%". That fight has not taken place.</p>

      <p>In a four-hour debate with the vegan YouTuber Vegan Gains, he struggled to say whether he supported laws against murder. The debate grew his channel considerably.</p>

      <h2>Money</h2>

      <p>Goatis has celebrated years of unemployment on camera; by his own count he hasn't worked since 2009. The documentary speculates that his income comes from disability benefits but can't prove it.</p>

      <p>What it can show is a long list of fundraisers:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>€5,000 for a girlfriend's medical treatment.</li>
        <li>About $4,000 for a "lost" camera that police then found.</li>
        <li>A legal fund to win custody of his son, which was quietly dropped.</li>
        <li>One Bitcoin, worth around $40,000 at the time, to attend a vegan protest that never happened. A week later he said he had been robbed in Kazakhstan.</li>
        <li>A request for €500,000 to sue Google after YouTube banned his channel.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Turkey Tom calls several of these grifts. Whether or not that's fair, very few appear to have produced what they promised.</p>

      <h2>The Airbnbs</h2>

      <p>Many of Goatis' travels were paid for by fans, often in fairly upscale Airbnbs, and several stays ended badly.</p>

      <p>In Kyiv in 2018, his host left a review calling him the worst guest she had ever had. She described eggs thrown at the walls and windows, broken dishes, appliances and toilets, nightly drunken parties reported by neighbours, and a guest who left without checking out, leaving the door open. Goatis said it was all lies and left a review of his own, complaining that there had been only one roll of toilet paper.</p>

      <p>In Romania, he set up a "commune" with a few subscribers in a €900-a-month Airbnb. It lasted a matter of months. On a livestream, he laughingly explained how it ended:</p>

      <blockquote>"So I took an Uber to Bulgaria and crossed the border and got out."</blockquote>

      <p>The landlady's account, the other residents' accounts and his own later version all differ wildly. The host's review mentions police called over an assault, a destroyed door, and his girlfriend and child left alone in Bucharest. Several of the residents said he eventually drove them out at knife point. He, in turn, accused one of them of planning far worse crimes, without evidence.</p>

      <p>Later, a video in which he claimed to be in Canada was undone by his own public Airbnb profile, which placed the house in the Netherlands. The profile went private shortly afterwards.</p>

      <h2>Relationships</h2>

      <p>This is where the documentary is at its darkest. It describes what it calls a pattern repeated across several partners: someone vulnerable is drawn in, then isolated and controlled. Testimonies mention passports, phones and money being kept from them. After the break-up comes public humiliation.</p>

      <p>After his relationship with Luna, the mother of his son, ended, Goatis accused her of abuse on video. He also published her real name and country, reported her to child protective services, and asked his subscribers to mass-report her. Unusually, his own audience largely refused. A channel then appeared uploading private videos of her. Similar channels later appeared for another ex, one of which was removed for impersonation.</p>

      <p>Former partners and hosts have, in turn, accused him of physical violence, threats with knives, and holding on to their documents. These accounts come from interviews, videos and court statements. They have not been tested in a trial of Goatis himself.</p>

      <p>A forum user also leaked a series of emails, which appear to come from an alternative account of his. In them he courts a woman while still in a relationship, insults her mother with a racial slur, and asks her for explicit photos. The documentary allows that the emails could be fake, but considers them entirely plausible.</p>

      <h2>Allegations involving minors</h2>

      <p>Some of the most serious claims in the documentary come from anonymous posters on the forum Kiwi Farms, and from a commenter who says she spoke to him online in 2009, when she was 13 and he was 19. They allege that he groomed teenage girls and asked them for photos. The documentary is explicit that these are unproven allegations backed only by the posters themselves, though it notes how often similar stories recur.</p>

      <p>What is not in dispute is a 2022 video of his own, "A Woman's Purpose". In it he argued that women should start having children from their first period, and that the age of consent is set unnaturally high. Even many of his remaining fans turned on him over it.</p>

      <h2>The acid attack and the arson (2022–2023)</h2>

      <p>In May 2022, a woman in the Netherlands was attacked with acid near her home. In January 2023, her house was set on fire. She was later identified as Luna.</p>

      <p>A man and his teenage daughter were arrested and later convicted. He received a prison sentence, and Luna was awarded €60,000 in damages. In court, the attacker said he had acted under threat, and he identified the person threatening him as Luna's former partner, a well-known vlogger.</p>

      <p>A former partner of Goatis also gave a written statement to the court, and has said in videos that he spoke about throwing acid in Luna's face and burning down her house. Internet sleuths geolocated footage of him to within a few hundred feet of the attack. He blames Luna for violence against him and his circle.</p>

      <p>As far as the documentary reports, Goatis himself has not been charged with anything in connection with the attacks. Its own opening and conclusion state his guilt more firmly than the evidence it presents in between.</p>

      <h2>Today</h2>

      <p>His original Sv3rige channel was banned from YouTube in 2022 without a public reason. He moved his archive to Telegram and continued on new channels, the current one being Goatis Reviews.</p>

      <p>He keeps his location secret; viewers have placed him in Kazakhstan and Thailand, among others. He has issued copyright strikes against creators who document his history, and has more recently taken a religious turn. Turkey Tom's documentary ends by calling on authorities to finally catch up with him, and with the observation that the Hafþór fight remains unscheduled.</p>

